    <section class="am" id="posi-askisi">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:4rem;">How much exercise is enough?</h2>
      <p class="lead">The World Health Organization recommends, for every adult, every week:</p>
      <div class="am-goals">
        <div class="am-goal"><span class="num">75–150’</span><span class="cap">of vigorous activity, such as running</span></div>
        <div class="am-goal"><span class="num">150–300’</span><span class="cap">of moderate activity, such as brisk walking</span></div>
        <div class="am-goal"><span class="num">2+</span><span class="cap">days of strength work for all major muscles</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="am-note">You can mix them: 1 minute of running counts about as much as 2 minutes of brisk walking. And if you're not at the goal yet, some movement is always better than none: in a large review, even running once a week, for under 50 minutes, was linked to fewer early deaths.</p>
      <form class="am-tool" id="amTool" novalidate>
        <h3>Count your week</h3>
        <div class="am-fields">
          <label for="amRun">Minutes of running
            <input type="number" id="amRun" min="0" max="3000" step="5" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="0">
          </label>
          <label for="amMod">Minutes of brisk walking, cycling, etc.
            <input type="number" id="amMod" min="0" max="3000" step="5" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="0">
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="am-bar" aria-hidden="true"><div class="am-fill" id="amFill"></div><span class="am-mark"></span></div>
        <div class="am-scale" aria-hidden="true"><span>0</span><span>150</span><span>300+</span></div>
        <p class="am-result" id="amText" aria-live="polite">Enter how many minutes you ran and walked briskly this week.</p>
        <p class="am-small">
          Your minutes are not stored anywhere. Sources:
          <a href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240015128" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO, 2020 guidelines ↗</a> ·
          <a href="https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/54/15/898" target="_blank" rel="noopener">British Journal of Sports Medicine, 2020 ↗</a>
        </p>
      </form>
    </section>

    <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:4rem;">What you gain after...</h2>
    <p class="lead">The benefits of running build up gradually. Here's what changes over time.</p>
    <p class="lead" style="font-size:0.85rem;margin-top:-0.6rem;">Tap a card to see why it happens.</p>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <button class="timeline-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <div class="timeline-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 12h4l3 8 4-16 3 8h4"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>1 run</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Better mood</li>
            <li>Less stress</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="timeline-chevron"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-more"><p>Why? Exercise makes your body release substances such as endorphins and endocannabinoids, which is why you feel better almost right away.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <button class="timeline-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <div class="timeline-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M16 2v4"/><path d="M8 2v4"/><path d="M3 10h18"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>1 month</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Better fitness level</li>
            <li>Less fatigue</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="timeline-chevron"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-more"><p>Why? Your heart and lungs start adapting to the extra load, so the same distance starts to feel easier.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <button class="timeline-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <div class="timeline-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M16 2v4"/><path d="M8 2v4"/><path d="M3 10h18"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>6 months</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Better cardiorespiratory endurance</li>
            <li>Lower resting heart rate</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="timeline-chevron"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-more"><p>Why? You build more capillaries and your heart pumps a greater volume of blood with every beat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-item">
        <button class="timeline-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <div class="timeline-icon">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M16 2v4"/><path d="M8 2v4"/><path d="M3 10h18"/></svg>
          </div>
          <h3>1 year</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Lower blood pressure</li>
            <li>Better cholesterol</li>
          </ul>
          <span class="timeline-chevron"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></span>
        </button>
        <div class="timeline-more"><p>Why? Month after month, regular exercise lowers blood pressure and improves cholesterol. And running has become a habit that supports you instead of wearing you down.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="am-small">Source: <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/benefits/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC, benefits of physical activity ↗</a></p>

    <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:4rem;">Did you know?</h2>
    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div class="fact-card">
        <p class="fact-label"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="9" r="6"/><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg> Knees</p>
        <p>Recreational running doesn't "wear out" your knees: 3.5% of recreational runners had knee or hip arthritis, compared with 10.2% of people who didn't exercise.</p>
        <a class="card-source" href="https://www.jospt.org/doi/10.2519/jospt.2017.7137" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source: JOSPT, 2017 ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <p class="fact-label"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="9" r="6"/><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg> Injuries</p>
        <p>In 25 studies with more than 26,000 participants, strength training cut injuries to less than a third. Stretching had no measurable effect.</p>
        <a class="card-source" href="https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/48/11/871" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source: British Journal of Sports Medicine, 2014 ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <p class="fact-label"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="9" r="6"/><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 21h4"/></svg> Longevity</p>
        <p>People who run, even a little, had 27% fewer deaths from any cause than people who don't run.</p>
        <a class="card-source" href="https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/54/15/898" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source: British Journal of Sports Medicine, 2020 ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="stop" id="when-to-stop">
      <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:4rem;">When to slow down or stop</h2>
      <p class="lead">Running is safe for most people. But learn the signs that say "that's enough for today".</p>
      <div class="stop-grid">
        <div class="stop-card stop-now">
          <h3>Stop now and call emergency services (112 in Europe)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Pain, pressure or tightness in the chest, especially if it spreads to an arm, the neck, jaw or back</li>
            <li>Fainting, or feeling like you're about to faint</li>
            <li>Sudden, severe breathlessness that doesn't ease with rest</li>
            <li>In the heat: confusion, hot skin that isn't sweating, or seizures (heatstroke)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="stop-card stop-rest">
          <h3>Stop for today</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Dizziness, nausea or a heartbeat that feels irregular</li>
            <li>Pain that makes you limp or gets worse as you run</li>
            <li>Sharp pain at one spot on a bone, or swelling</li>
            <li>Fever, a chesty cough, aches all over or an upset stomach</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If the dizziness, chest pain or irregular heartbeat comes back, talk to a doctor before you run again.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stop-card stop-slow">
          <h3>Slow down</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>On an easy run you can't say a full sentence</li>
            <li>You only have a runny nose or a mild sore throat: run shorter and slower</li>
            <li>It's hot and humid: see how much to slow down in <a href="kairos-rythmos-en.html">Weather-Adjusted Pace</a></li>
            <li>You're sleeping badly or your legs have felt heavy for days</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stop-before">
        <h3>Talk to a doctor before you start if:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>you have heart disease, high blood pressure, diabetes or kidney disease</li>
          <li>you've had chest pain, dizziness or fainting when you exert yourself</li>
          <li>you take medicine for your heart or blood pressure</li>
          <li>you're pregnant or have recently given birth</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The <a href="https://eparmedx.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PAR-Q+ ↗</a> questionnaire helps you check in a few minutes whether you need a check-up first.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="am-small">
        This information does not replace medical advice. Sources:
        <a href="https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/heart-attack/warning-signs-of-a-heart-attack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Heart Association ↗</a> ·
        <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heat-exhaustion-heatstroke/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHS, heatstroke ↗</a> ·
        <a href="https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/fitness/expert-answers/exercise/faq-20058494" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mayo Clinic, running with a cold ↗</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:4rem;">Small habits, big difference</h2>
    <ul class="tips">
      <li>
        <strong>Sleep = training</strong>
        Your body grows stronger while it rests, not while it runs. Most adults need 7–9 hours of sleep.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hydrate before you're thirsty</strong>
        Drink water throughout the day, not just around your run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Warm up by moving, not stretching</strong>
        Start with 5 minutes of brisk walking or very easy running. Stretching doesn't seem to prevent injuries; do it afterwards if it feels good. What really protects you is strength training.
      </li>
    </ul>

